{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Domain requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a fenced citation with commentary. The built-in allows that on an explicitly declared function with a body. It excuses only that function's zero execution.\n\nThe commentary gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty. `Domain` requires the callback. The storage-only fixture `StoredGraph` is meant to delegate through `node_path` alone. Edge discovery would be an unwanted graph access, so the body is a deliberate `panic!` guard. Execution of that guard would mean the test failed. The commentary also says traversal fixtures exercise edge discovery elsewhere.\n\nThe commentary agrees with the current source:\n- The `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` delegations in `impl[StoredGraph/Domain]` call only `self.0.load` and `self.0.store` with `self.node_path(node)`.\n- The delegation test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` and the validator test `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` use only these storage paths and never reach edge discovery. I did not read the validator's code. I am inferring that `verify` never calls `outgoing_edges`, because the tests would panic if it did.\n- The function's body is exactly the panic guard the commentary describes.\n\nI found no gap in the citation.",
